result:
All 25 UDS tests pass.

## What was wrong

`std::os::unix::net::SocketAddr::from_abstract_name` takes the abstract name **without** the leading NUL — it writes the NUL into `sun_path` itself. Both call sites detected the `\0` prefix and then passed the whole slice through, NUL included:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:83` — `UnixListener::bind`
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:79` — `UnixStream::connect`

So binding `"\0aaa"` registered the abstract name `\0aaa`, i.e. `sun_path` = `\0\0aaa`. Connect had the identical off-by-one, so the two agreed with each other and the connection succeeded — which is why the bug stayed silent. It only surfaces on inspection: `local_addr().as_abstract_name()` returned `[0, 97, 97]` instead of `[97, 97, 97]`. It also means the name is unreachable from any correct peer (std, or another language's client) that binds/connects to the same string.

## The fix

Strip the prefix and pass only the name, using `strip_prefix` so the check and the slice can't drift apart:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
// `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself, so
// it must be given the name without it.
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verified both directions: with the fix the test passes; stashing it reproduces `left: [0, 97, 97], right: [97, 97, 97]`.

Two notes on scope — those were the only two `from_abstract_name` call sites in the tree, and I left `UnixDatagram` alone since it routes through std's own `bind`/`connect` rather than this path, so it isn't affected by this bug. Changes are unstaged; I haven't committed.
